KG42.register("rules-0ac326854f0390a372f5b377",{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R004","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R004","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Counterparty matching","process_stage":"matching","market_meaning":"ICT infrastructure and contractual arrangements may limit which participants can negotiate flexibility transactions with one another. Scope: Potential counterparties in the networked distribution-system flexibility market. The networked arrangement permits the DSO to negotiate with intermediary aggregators and each aggregator to negotiate with certain prosumers. Scope: Counterparty permissions in the proposed networked flexibility market. Direct prosumer-to-DSO trading can be incorporated when aggregators are not required. Scope: Optional direct counterparty arrangement within the proposed flexibility market. Condition: Aggregators are not required. The possible extension could include prosumer-to-prosumer transactions, allowing prosumers to negotiate with peers to fulfil previously negotiated flexibility obligations. Scope: Possible extension beyond the day-ahead trading considered in the paper. Condition: Additional trading closer to real-time is added.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function matching.counterparties under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["matching.counterparties"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Counterparty permissions in the proposed networked flexibility market. | Optional direct counterparty arrangement within the proposed flexibility market. | Possible extension beyond the day-ahead trading considered in the paper. | Potential counterparties in the networked distribution-system flexibility market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01","v141-b-g01-E06","v141-b-g01-E11"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"ICT infrastructure and contractual arrangements may limit which participants can negotiate flexibility transactions with one another. Scope: Potential counterparties in the networked distribution-system flexibility market.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The networked arrangement permits the DSO to negotiate with intermediary aggregators and each aggregator to negotiate with certain prosumers. Scope: Counterparty permissions in the proposed networked flexibility market.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E06"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Direct prosumer-to-DSO trading can be incorporated when aggregators are not required. Scope: Optional direct counterparty arrangement within the proposed flexibility market. Condition: Aggregators are not required.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E06"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The possible extension could include prosumer-to-prosumer transactions, allowing prosumers to negotiate with peers to fulfil previously negotiated flexibility obligations. Scope: Possible extension beyond the day-ahead trading considered in the paper. Condition: Additional trading closer to real-time is added.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E11"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E01","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p02-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction; design requirements","anchor_text":"to obtain ﬂexibility [42]. ng In [43], it is recognized that within existing electricity mar- o- ket","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"This paper introduces a decentralized market design, which allows a DSO to manage local demand constraints by obtaining flexibility from competing aggregators, which must in-turn incentivize prosumers to provide this flexibility. The novel networked market structure accounts for DSO-to-aggregator and aggregator-to-prosumer ICT infrastructure and contractual arrangements, which may limit which participants can negotiate transactions with one another."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"p gg g q g the paper, the DSO, prosumers and aggregators participating in m the market are generical","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"It is assumed that the DSO is responsible for ensuring the total demand in the distribution network does not exceed a maximum limit. The proposed flexibility market design allows the DSO to incentivize demand flexibility from local prosumers to achieve this. ICT infrastructure and contractual agreements may be needed to facilitate the procurement of flexibility. Therefore, rather than requiring the DSO to negotiate directly with each prosumer, the proposed flexibility market is designed based on a networked structure, with the DSO able to negotiate with intermediary aggregators, and each aggregator in-turn able to negotiate with certain prosumers. Direct prosumer-to-DSO trading can also be incorporated if aggregators are not required. Throughout the paper, the DSO, prosumers and aggregators participating in the market are generically referred to as agents."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Only day-ahead trading is considered, but additional trading closer to real-time could be added, allowing agents to renegotiate based on updated demand predictions. In this case, prosumer-to-prosumer transactions could be included, allowing prosumers to negotiate with peers to fulfil their previously negotiated flexibility obligations."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":"This is a qualitative counterparty-permission rule. The supplied evidence does not define a decision variable for which counterparties are permitted; it states permissions as market-structure prose. No x is declared rather than importing variables from the clearing or pricing rules.","empty_theta_reason":"No numeric or fixed parameter is specified for counterparty permissions in the supplied evidence. The set and agent symbols appearing elsewhere define the general trading network, not a fixed parameter of this rule. No theta is declared."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E01","quote":"The novel networked market structure accounts for DSO-to-aggregator and aggregator-to-prosumer ICT infrastructure and contractual arrangements, which may limit which participants can negotiate transactions with one another."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E06","quote":"the proposed flexibility market is designed based on a networked structure, with the DSO able to negotiate with intermediary aggregators, and each aggregator in-turn able to negotiate with certain prosumers."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E06","quote":"Direct prosumer-to-DSO trading can also be incorporated if aggregators are not required."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E11","quote":"Only day-ahead trading is considered, but additional trading closer to real-time could be added, allowing agents to renegotiate based on updated demand predictions. In this case, prosumer-to-prosumer transactions could be included, allowing prosumers to negotiate with peers to fulfil their previously negotiated flexibility obligations."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"bad44a5909defe7cd9befe481c30fdbafe330743df27e8414144f63f4f5d58c7","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"The supplied rule states counterparty permissions and scope conditions in prose: ICT/contractual arrangements may restrict which participants negotiate, the networked structure allows DSO-to-aggregator and aggregator-to-prosumer negotiation, direct DSO-to-prosumer trading is conditional on aggregators not being required, and prosumer-to-prosumer transactions are only a possible real-time extension. No equation, inequality, optimization objective, decision variable, or fixed parameter is supplied for this counterparty-permission relation. The general trading-network symbols appearing elsewhere in the source define contract-market mechanics rather than this rule's x/theta. The four C.1 constraints therefore remain procedural and not_formalized. B evidence remains unreviewed under the experimental policy.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","node_ids":["matching.counterparties"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C001 is a qualitative limit on admissible negotiating pairs and attaches to the frozen matching.counterparties node; no edge exists in this rule."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","node_ids":["matching.counterparties"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C002 describes the baseline allowed DSO-aggregator and aggregator-prosumer links and attaches to matching.counterparties; no edge exists in this rule."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-C003","node_ids":["matching.counterparties"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C003 is the conditional direct DSO-prosumer variant of the same matching permission and attaches to matching.counterparties; no edge exists in this rule."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-C004","node_ids":["matching.counterparties"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C004 is the conditional prosumer-prosumer extension of the same matching permission and attaches to matching.counterparties; no edge exists in this rule."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
